            <!-- ---------- THESIS ---------- -->
            <section class="forming-thesis" aria-label="The short answer">
              <span class="forming-thesis__label">The short answer</span>
              <p>
                A mold fee is not a markup on your order. It is the price of a
                physical object — a block of hardened steel with your design cut
                into it — that is made once and used for every unit you will ever
                order. That single fact explains why the fee is quoted
                separately, why it does not fall when you double the quantity,
                why it is charged again when the artwork changes, and why the
                question of who owns it matters more than the number on the line.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 01 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="forming"
              class="forming-section"
              data-article-section="forming"
            >
              <header class="forming-section__header">
                <span class="forming-section__index">01</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="forming-section__eyebrow">Process</p>
                  <h2>Two ways to move metal</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Every custom metal piece starts as either a flat sheet that gets
                deformed, or a cavity that gets filled. Stamping pushes a blank
                between a punch and a die; the metal flows into the shape and
                keeps its thickness. Die-casting injects molten zinc alloy into a
                closed steel mold; the part takes the shape of the cavity and is
                ejected when it solidifies.
              </p>

              <p>
                The choice is usually made by the drawing, not by the price. A
                design that is essentially a plate with relief — a pin, a badge, a
                tag — belongs to stamping. A design that is a small sculpture
                with depth, hollows and curved sides belongs to casting. Trying
                to force one into the other process is where most avoidable
                tooling cost comes from.
              </p>

