                Buyers picture a die as one solid block. It is an assembly. A
                stamping die is a stack of plates: the upper shoe carries the
                punch, the stripper holds the sheet flat while the punch enters,
                the die block contains the cavity, and the lower shoe sits on the
                press bed. Guide pillars keep the two halves aligned to a few
                hundredths of a millimetre, stroke after stroke.
              </p>

