              <p>
                This is why a small change to the drawing can cost real money.
                Moving a line by 0.2 mm is a file edit; moving it in hardened
                steel means re-cutting the cavity, re-making the electrode, and
                re-fitting the shut-off. The fee follows the steel, not the
                drawing.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-note">
                <p class="forming-note__label">Relief levels are tooling stations</p>
                <p class="forming-note__foot">
                  A single-level design needs one forming surface. A two-level
                  design — a raised border around a recessed field — needs a
                  second strike or a second station. A sculpted, multi-level
                  relief needs more. When a quote for the "same" badge comes back
                  noticeably higher, count the levels before questioning the
                  price.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 03 ---------- -->
            <section id="fee" class="forming-section" data-article-section="fee">
              <header class="forming-section__header">
                <span class="forming-section__index">03</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="forming-section__eyebrow">Cost</p>
                  <h2>What the mold fee is made of</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                The fee is an engineering job, not a material purchase. Steel is
                a minority of it. Most of the money is machine hours and skilled
                handwork, which is why a tool cannot be discounted the way a
                production run can — there is no material substitution that makes
                it cheaper without changing what you get.
              </p>

              <ul class="forming-bars">
                {costRows.map((row) => (
                  <li>
                    <div class="forming-bars__head">
                      <span class="forming-bars__label">{row.label}</span>
                      <span class="forming-bars__share">{row.share}</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="forming-bars__track">
                      <span
                        class="forming-bars__fill"
                        style={`width:${row.width}%`}
                      ></span>
                    </div>
                    <p class="forming-bars__note">{row.note}</p>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>

              <p class="forming-bars__caption">
                Typical split for a small-part tool, not a fixed formula. Shares
                move with cavity count, relief depth and how much hand-fitting the
                geometry demands.
              </p>

              <p>
                This is also where two quotes for the “same” die quietly diverge.
                One shop runs three EDM passes and fits the shut-off by hand; the
                other runs one pass and relies on the machine. One hardens the
                cavity and tempers it; the other skips the step because it adds a
                week. Both produce a tool that works on the first day. They stop
                being comparable somewhere in the second year — which is the
                subject of sections 06 and 07.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 04 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="amortisation"
              class="forming-section"
              data-article-section="amortisation"
            >
              <header class="forming-section__header">
                <span class="forming-section__index">04</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="forming-section__eyebrow">Economics</p>
                  <h2>Why the fee doesn't scale</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                A mold fee is a fixed cost. It is paid once, before the first
                good part exists, and it does not care how many parts follow. The
                arithmetic is simple: cost per unit equals the tooling divided by
                the quantity, plus the piece price.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-formula">
                <span class="forming-formula__part">cost per unit</span>
                <span class="forming-formula__op">=</span>
                <span class="forming-formula__frac">
                  <span>tooling</span>
                  <span>quantity</span>
                </span>
                <span class="forming-formula__op">+</span>
                <span class="forming-formula__part">piece price</span>
              </div>

              <p>
                Only the first term moves. Doubling the order halves the tooling
                share; it does not touch the piece price. That is why the same
                fee feels enormous on a first sample order and irrelevant on a
                repeat order ten times the size.
              </p>

              <figure class="forming-figure">
                <div class="forming-figure__frame">
                  <svg
                    viewBox="0 0 660 214"
