                      10×
                    </text>

                    <text
                      x="70"
                      y="32"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      tooling share of unit cost
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="612"
                      y="208"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="end"
                    >
                      order quantity
                    </text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <figcaption>
                  Shape of the curve, not a price list. The share collapses fast,
                  then flattens — and the flattening is where the fee stops
                  mattering.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <div class="forming-table-wrap">
                <table class="forming-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Quantity</th>
                      <th scope="col">Tooling share</th>
                      <th scope="col">How it reads on the invoice</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {scaleRows.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <td>{row.multiple}</td>
                        <td>{row.share}</td>
                        <td>{row.reading}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p>
                The practical consequence: a tool is expensive only if you never
                reorder. A program that runs three seasons pays for its tooling
                several times over, and the second order carries no fee at all.
                When you compare suppliers, compare the cost of the first order
                plus the reorders — not the tooling line in isolation.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 05 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="limits"
              class="forming-section"
              data-article-section="limits"
            >
              <header class="forming-section__header">
                <span class="forming-section__index">05</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="forming-section__eyebrow">Design</p>
                  <h2>What each path lets you draw</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                The two processes are not interchangeable, and the differences
                are geometric rather than aesthetic. If a design needs a
                paper-thin edge, it cannot be cast. If it needs a hollow that
                would trap the part in the mold, it cannot be stamped without
                extra slides or a redesign.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-table-wrap">
                <table class="forming-table forming-table--limits">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Constraint</th>
                      <th scope="col">Stamped</th>
                      <th scope="col">Die-cast</th>
                      <th scope="col">Why</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {limitRows.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <td>{row.limit}</td>
                        <td>{row.stamping}</td>
                        <td>{row.casting}</td>
                        <td>{row.why}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p>
                In practice the decision is made in the first sketch. Flat with
                relief, sharp edges, fine line work: stamping. Dimensional, thick,
                curved, with hollows: casting. A design that sits between the two
                — a thick body with fine engraved detail — usually resolves as
                casting plus a stamped insert, or as stamping with a simpler
                silhouette.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 06 ---------- -->
            <section id="life" class="forming-section" data-article-section="life">
              <header class="forming-section__header">
                <span class="forming-section__index">06</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="forming-section__eyebrow">Durability</p>
                  <h2>How long a tool actually lasts</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Tool life is quoted as a range, never a number, because it depends
                on what the tool is asked to do. Sheet hardness, relief depth,
                press condition, lubrication and the maintenance interval all move
                it. A die that runs thin brass with shallow relief will outlast a
                die cutting deep relief in stainless by a wide margin — same
                supplier, same steel.
              </p>

              <p>
                What matters more than the headline figure is the order in which
                a tool degrades. Precision does not collapse; it drifts, and it
                drifts in a predictable sequence.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-ladder">
                {wearSteps.map((item) => (
                  <div class="forming-ladder__step">
                    <span class="forming-ladder__num">{item.step}</span>
                    <div>
                      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                      <p>{item.text}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                Two tools can both be described as “a die” and behave completely
                differently at this stage. One still holds the fine separations
                after a long run; the other has already rounded them, and the
                enamel that used to sit in a crisp channel now sits in a soft one.
                The part still functions. It just no longer matches the sample
                anyone approved.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-note">
                <p class="forming-note__label">Maintenance is part of the life</p>
                <p class="forming-note__foot">
                  Polishing the cavity, re-fitting the shut-off, replacing ejector
                  pins and re-cutting worn inserts all restore precision. A
                  maintained tool can run far past its nominal life; an
                  unmaintained one drifts early and is then blamed on the design.
                  Ask for the maintenance interval and the log, not just the
                  expected shot count.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 07 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="our-tooling"
              class="forming-section"
              data-article-section="our-tooling"
            >
              <header class="forming-section__header">
                <span class="forming-section__index">07</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="forming-section__eyebrow">Our workshop</p>
                  <h2>Why our tooling lasts longer</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                A longer tool life is not a promise made at the end of a
                quotation. It is a set of decisions made before the first cut,
                most of which are invisible in a photograph of the finished die.
                These are the five we hold ourselves to, and each one has a
                specific effect on how slowly the tool loses precision.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-decisions">
                {decisions.map((item) => (
                  <div class="forming-decision">
                    <span class="forming-decision__num">{item.num}</span>
                    <div class="forming-decision__body">
                      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                      <p>{item.text}</p>
                      <p class="forming-decision__effect">
                        <span>Effect</span>
                        {item.effect}
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                The difference shows up as time, not as a specification. The first
                re-polish comes later. The fine lines in the artwork survive more
                cycles before they soften. The shut-off stays clean, so parts stop
                needing de-flashing by hand. And when the tool does need work,
                replacing an insert restores the geometry instead of accepting a
                worn cavity.
              </p>

              <p>
                It also shows up in what we can hand over. Every tool has a
                record: steel grade, hardness, cavity count, the maintenance
                interval, and the first-article dimensions measured at T1. Those
                numbers are what make a claim about tool life checkable rather
                than rhetorical — for us and for anyone else quoting you.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-note">
                <p class="forming-note__label">A question that separates quotes</p>
                <p class="forming-note__foot">
                  Ask any supplier for the steel grade and the cavity hardness of
                  the tool they are quoting. If the answer is vague, the claim
                  about durability has nothing behind it — because those two
                  values decide how fast the edge rounds, long before any
                  marketing sentence does.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 08 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="ownership"
              class="forming-section"
              data-article-section="ownership"
            >
              <header class="forming-section__header">
                <span class="forming-section__index">08</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="forming-section__eyebrow">Ownership</p>
                  <h2>Who owns the tool</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                In most contracts the party that pays for the tool owns it. The
                complication is that ownership is legal while possession is
                physical: the tool lives in a factory, on a shelf, in a rack with
                hundreds of others. What you actually own is a right to have it
                used, maintained, and — if you leave — handed over.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-timeline">
                <div class="forming-timeline__step">
                  <span class="forming-timeline__dot"></span>
                  <h3>Quote</h3>
                  <p>
                    Agree what the fee covers, whether it is one-time, and whether
                    it is refundable against a volume commitment.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="forming-timeline__step">
                  <span class="forming-timeline__dot"></span>
                  <h3>Tool built</h3>
                  <p>
                    Approve the T1 part and record the dimensions. This becomes
                    the reference for every later inspection.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="forming-timeline__step">
                  <span class="forming-timeline__dot"></span>
                  <h3>First run</h3>
                  <p>
                    Confirm the storage terms, the maintenance interval, and who
                    pays for wear-related repair.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div class="forming-timeline__step">
                  <span class="forming-timeline__dot"></span>
                  <h3>Reorder</h3>
                  <p>
                    No new fee, but check the reorder minimum and whether the tool
                    has been maintained since the last run.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>

              <p>
                “Free tooling” is the phrase to slow down on. It usually means one
                of three things: the fee is amortised into the unit price, so you
                pay it anyway; the fee is waived only against a volume commitment
                you may not reach; or the tool is not yours, so it cannot travel
                with you if you change suppliers. None of these is automatically
                bad — but each one should be named in the contract rather than
                discovered later.
              </p>

              <h3 class="forming-subhead">Six questions to settle before you pay</h3>

              <ol class="forming-questions">
                {questions.map((item) => (
                  <li>{item}</li>
                ))}
              </ol>

              <p>
                A supplier who answers all six without hesitation is telling you
                something about how the tool will be treated in year three. One
                who deflects is telling you the same thing.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- CLOSING ---------- -->
            <footer class="forming-conclusion">
              <div class="forming-conclusion__rule"></div>
              <p class="forming-conclusion__label">The decision</p>

              <div class="forming-rule">
                <p>
                  <strong>Flat with relief, sharp edges, repeat orders.</strong>
                  Stamp it, and treat the die as an asset you will use for years.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Dimensional, thick, sculpted, short run.</strong>
                  Cast it, and accept that the tool cost sits heavily on the first
                  order.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Unsure which one the drawing wants.</strong> Send the
                  artwork before the quantity — the shape decides the process, and
                  the process decides the fee.
                </p>
              </div>

              <p class="forming-conclusion__text">
                If you already have a quotation with a tooling line on it, send it
                over. Reading where the money went is usually faster than
                starting the brief again — and it tells you whether the tool you
                are paying for will still hold your artwork in three years.
              </p>

              <div class="forming-conclusion__signature">
                <span>METALCRAFTS</span>
                <span>CRAFT · DETAIL · QUALITY</span>
              </div>

